WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:34.000
This is 99% Invisible. I'm Roman Mars. When you go to a concert, you might try to get there right when the doors open. Or maybe you take your time and skip the opening act. But generally, you want to be there when the show starts. In February, everyone who went to a concert in Haberstadt, Germany showed up 23 years late. Alright. Getting ready to head to the train station. One of those late comers was Gabe Bullard. Gabe's a reporter living in Basel, Switzerland.
这里是 99% Invisible。我是罗曼·马尔斯。当你去听音乐会时，你可能会试着在门刚开的时候就赶到。或者你可能会慢条斯理，错过开场暖场表演。但总的来说，你希望在演出开始时人在现场。今年二月，所有去德国哈尔贝施塔特听音乐会的人，都迟到了整整 23 年。好吧。准备前往火车站。其中一位迟到者是加布·布拉德。加布是一名住在瑞士巴塞尔的记者。

2
00:00:34.000 --> 00:01:04.000
So he took the train a few hours into Germany to a pretty rural part of the country. Next stop, Alberstadt. Alberstadt is an old medieval town, like what you'd expect from a storybook. There are timber-frame houses packed close together, curving cobblestone streets. But there wasn't time for sightseeing. The concert was well underway. And Gabe was planning to leave early, because from this concert, everyone walks out early.
于是他坐了几个小时的火车进入德国，来到了一个相当乡村的地区。下一站，哈尔贝施塔特。哈尔贝施塔特是一座中世纪古镇，就像你从童话书里想象的那样。那里有紧密排列的木构房屋，以及弯曲的鹅卵石街道。但没有时间去观光了。音乐会已经在进行中了。而且加布打算提前离开，因为这场音乐会，所有人都会提前退场。

3
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:38.000
It's a performance of a piece called Organ 2 ASLSP. ASLSP stands for As Slow As Possible, which is how the composer meant for it to be played. And that's how it is being played. Very slowly. And the reason I came all the way to Halberstadt now is because this was no ordinary day of the performance. I came to see a chord change. The last time Organ 2 ASLSP had a chord change was 2022, and this new chord will play until the next change in August of 2026. There's a change the
这是一场名为《管风琴 2/ASLSP》的作品的演出。ASLSP 代表“尽可能慢”，这也是作曲家希望它的演奏方式。事实也确实如此。非常缓慢。而我大老远跑到哈尔贝施塔特的原因是，今天可不是普通的一天演出。我是来看和弦转换的。《管风琴 2/ASLSP》上一次和弦转换是在 2022 年，这个新和弦将一直持续演奏到 2026 年 8 月的下一次转换。之后的那一年

4
00:01:38.000 --> 00:02:21.000
year after that and the year after that and so on until the year 2640. The full performance is meant to last 639 years. Walking up to the concert, looks like this is it. Gonna walk in. ["The Church of a Nun's Cloister"] The performance is in the Church of a Nun's Cloister, founded in the 13th century. It's an old building, with bare stone walls, a floor that's mostly gravel, and a wood roof. When I went in, there were a few people looking at what I realized was the organ.
还有再后一年，以此类推，直到 2640 年。完整的演出预计持续 639 年。走到音乐会现场，看起来就是这里了。准备走进去。[《修女修道院教堂》] 演出地点在布尔卡迪修女修道院教堂，建于 13 世纪。这是一座古老的建筑，有着光秃秃的石墙，地面主要是砾石，还有木制屋顶。我进去的时候，有几个人正在看我后来意识到是管风琴的东西。

5
00:02:21.000 --> 00:03:06.000
But it didn't look like a typical organ. In the middle of the room here, there's a wooden structure with two kind of towers and then some metal pipes coming out of it. And they are making the sound. Those pipes are making the sound that's filling this room. One sound. And that's filling this room. One sound. And that's it. That's the performance. Well, at least until the chord change. At which point the organ will play a different one sound. Over the last 23 years, this tiny pipe organ playing this loud drone has drawn in thousands of fans, especially during chord changes.
但它看起来不像是典型的管风琴。房间正中间这里，有一个木制结构，带两个类似塔楼的东西，然后有一些金属管伸出来。它们正在发出声音。那些管子发出的声音充满了整个房间。只有一个声音。而且填满了这个房间。只有一个声音。就是这样。这就是演出。好吧，至少在和弦转换之前是这样。届时管风琴会演奏另一个单一的声音。在过去的 23 年里，这台演奏着响亮持续音的小型管风琴吸引了成千上万的乐迷，特别是在和弦转换期间。

6
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:38.000
And as unassuming and discordant as it is, it tends to keep people drawn in once they hear it. There are people who come for every change, often traveling from thousands of miles away. People who will stay in the church all day, just listening. People whom Oregon 2 ASLSP literally brings to tears. I came to Halberstadt to find out why. As in, why does Oregon 2 ASLSP even exist in the first place? And what is it that so mesmerizes people
尽管它毫不起眼且有些刺耳，但人们一旦听到，往往会被它深深吸引。有些人每次转换都会来，通常是从数千英里外赶来。有些人会整天待在教堂里，只是静静聆听。还有人听了《管风琴 2/ASLSP》后甚至感动落泪。我来到哈尔贝施塔特就是为了弄清原因。也就是说，《管风琴 2/ASLSP》当初为什么会存在？究竟是什么让人们如此着迷于

7
00:03:38.000 --> 00:04:31.000
about being in a cold, crumbling building listening to this? ["The Last Supper"] building, listening to this. The artist behind this very, very slow performance was John Cage. Cage was an American composer who started his career in the 1930s with well-received instrumental compositions like In a Landscape from 1948. Cage's compositions could be complex and modern, but they didn't always push boundaries. That changed in the 1950s, when he began experimenting not with the sound of music, but with the idea of music. His most famous piece is probably 4 minutes 33 seconds, during which the performer is
在一个寒冷、斑驳的建筑里听这个？[《最后的晚餐》] 建筑里听这个。这场极其、极其缓慢的表演背后的艺术家是约翰·凯奇。凯奇是一位美国作曲家，他的职业生涯始于 20 世纪 30 年代，创作了一些广受好评的器乐作品，比如 1948 年的《在风景中》。凯奇的作品可能很复杂且具有现代感，但它们并不总是突破界限。这种情况在 50 年代发生了变化，当时他开始尝试的不是音乐的声音，而是音乐的概念。他最著名的作品可能是《4 分 33 秒》，在表演期间，演奏者

8
00:04:31.000 --> 00:05:09.000
supposed to sit silent for 4 minutes and 33 seconds while the audience listens to whatever sounds are in the room, the air conditioner humming, other people shuffling uncomfortably, the traffic passing by outside. I love the activity of sound. Here he is talking about it in 1991. If you listen to Beethoven or to Mozart, you see that they're always the same. But if you listen to traffic, you see it's always different. You can read all sorts of meaning into Cage's work.
应该静坐 4 分 33 秒，而听众则聆听房间里的任何声音：空调的嗡嗡声、其他人不安的挪动声、外面驶过的交通声。我热爱声音的活动。这里是他在 1991 年谈论它。如果你听贝多芬或莫扎特，你会发现它们总是相同的。但如果你听交通噪音，你会发现它总是不同的。你可以在凯奇的作品中解读出各种各样的含义。

9
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:51.000
It's supposed to make us think about silence or randomness. Or maybe it's not really supposed to make us think much at all. In an interview, Cage quoted the philosopher Immanuel Kant. There are two things that don't have to mean anything. One is music and the other is laughter. Don't have to mean anything that is in order to give us very deep pleasure. In 1985, Cage experimented with time and wrote the first iteration of ASLSP with the explicit instruction to try to play it as slowly as possible.
它本意是让我们思考寂静或随机性。或者也许它根本不需要让我们想太多。在一次采访中，凯奇引用了哲学家伊曼纽尔·康德的话。有两样东西是不需要有任何含义的。一个是音乐，另一个是笑声。不需要有任何含义，也就是说，它们是为了给我们带来极深的愉悦。1985 年，凯奇对时间进行了实验，写出了《ASLSP》的第一个版本，并明确指示要尽量慢地演奏它。

10
00:05:51.000 --> 00:06:58.000
Here's one of the early attempts. Now, if you're thinking that the letters ASLSP don't really correspond to the words as slow as possible, Cage is one step ahead of you. The title is also a reference to a line in James Joyce's novel Finnegan's Wake. The line is, Soft morning city, Lisp, where Lisp is just spelled L-S-P. What that has to do with the music isn't really clear. But as more musicians began to take an interest in ASLSP, very soon performers trying to follow Cage's instructions hit a wall. Cage wrote this first iteration to be performed on piano, but there's a physical limit to how long a note can sound on a piano. You press a key, a hammer strikes a string, but the string will eventually stop vibrating.
这是早期的一次尝试。现在，如果你认为字母 ASLSP 和“As Slow As Possible（尽可能慢）”这几个词并不完全对应，那凯奇比你领先了一步。这个标题也是对詹姆斯·乔伊斯的小说《芬尼根的守灵夜》中一句台词的致敬。那句台词是：温柔的清晨之城，口齿不清（Soft morning city, Lisp），这里的 Lisp 只是拼写为 L-S-P。这和音乐有什么关系其实并不清楚。但随着越来越多的音乐家开始对《ASLSP》产生兴趣，很快，试图遵循凯奇指示的演奏者们就遇到了瓶颈。凯奇将这首初版作品写成由钢琴演奏，但钢琴上一个音符能响多久是有物理极限的。你按下琴键，琴锤敲击琴弦，但琴弦最终会停止振动。

11
00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:46.000
So the first performances of ASLSP lasted between 10 and 20 minutes, which is pretty long for a song. Just not, you know, long, long. Performers were stymied. They wanted a way to play ASLSP slower, much slower than was possible on a piano. But then, in 1987, Cage reworked the music for an instrument that didn't have any such limitations. That's Klaus Ad Heinrich playing. He's on the board of trustees for the project that brought Cage's ASLSP to Halberstadt. And he's director of music for the Halberstadt Cathedral, whose organ you're hearing. It's across town from the Cage project, and it's where I went to understand why the pipe organ
所以《ASLSP》的早期演出持续了 10 到 20 分钟，对于一首歌来说这已经挺长的了。只不过，你知道的，算不上真正意义上的长。演奏者们陷入了困境。他们想要一种以更慢的方式演奏《ASLSP》的方法，比在钢琴上可能的速度慢得多。但在 1987 年，凯奇为一件没有任何此类限制的乐器重新编排了这首音乐。那是克劳斯·阿德·海因里希在演奏。他是将凯奇的《ASLSP》带到哈尔贝施塔特的项目的理事会成员。他还是哈尔贝施塔特大教堂的音乐总监，你现在听到的就是该教堂的管风琴。它位于凯奇项目所在城镇的另一端，我去那里是为了弄清楚为什么管风琴

12
00:07:46.000 --> 00:08:14.000
is the perfect instrument for a performance that needs to last a very long time. The cathedral's organ is on a big stone balcony that rises above the main floor. To get there, we went through a wooden door and up twisting stone stairs with uneven grooves that have worn in over the centuries. At the top, Clauzade and I were in a stone area lit by the moonlight coming in from a gigantic ornate window.
是需要持续很长时间的演出的完美乐器。大教堂的管风琴位于主楼上方的一个大石制阳台上。为了到达那里，我们穿过一扇木门，沿着蜿蜒的石头楼梯向上走，楼梯上有几个世纪以来磨损出的不平整凹槽。在顶部，克劳斯·阿德和我来到一个石制区域，这里被从一扇巨大的华丽窗户透进来的月光照亮。

13
00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:58.000
Oh wow. We're inside the organ here. Impressive sight. You can look down there. This was my first time being inside a working pipe organ, and my first thought was that the name organ fits. Like a bodily organ, a pipe organ is an intricate system. And this system is all about moving air. And only air. Which is also the reason an organ can play much, much longer notes than a piano. Its sound doesn't come from a vibrating string. It comes from airflow. The air is drawn from whatever space the organ happens to be in. In this case, it came from inside the cathedral itself.
哦，哇。我们现在在管风琴内部了。真是令人印象深刻的景象。你可以往下看。这是我第一次进入一个运转中的管风琴内部，我的第一个想法是“管风琴（organ）”这个名字真贴切。就像身体的器官一样，管风琴是一个复杂的系统。而这个系统完全是为了输送空气。而且只有空气。这也是管风琴能够奏出比钢琴长得多、多得多的音符的原因。它的声音不是来自振动的琴弦。它来自气流。空气是从管风琴碰巧所在的任何空间中抽取进来的。在这个例子中，它来自大教堂内部本身。

14
00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:35.000
Klaus Erhard showed me an opening with a screen over it. That's to prevent birds from going in and not coming out again. Sometimes they let them fall and then they are inside and they die. From the cathedral's interior, the air goes into the organ through the bellows. These are basically big pumps. In the old days, bellows had to be manually pushed, sometimes by people standing on them and working them like a big medieval stair master. But in modern organs, like the one I was climbing around in, the process
克劳斯·埃尔哈德向我展示了一个带网罩的开口。那是为了防止鸟类飞进去却出不来。有时候它们会掉下去，然后在里面死掉。从大教堂内部，空气通过风箱进入管风琴。这些基本上是大泵。在过去，风箱必须手动推动，有时需要人们站在上面，像踩大型中世纪登阶机一样操作它们。但在现代管风琴中，就像我正在攀爬的这台，这个过程

15
00:09:35.000 --> 00:10:16.000
has been electrified. Klaus Erhardt went over to turn on a switch. Is that snow? I don't think so. They're rising. Yeah. From the bellows, we passed through another door into a room full of pipes standing in orderly rows. Then we went up a series of increasingly narrow ladders and stairs, climbing higher and higher into more rooms with more pipes. Some were wider than a bowling ball, while others were barely bigger than a pencil. The way the air from the bellows becomes music is by passing through these pipes. Every pipe
已经电动化了。克劳斯·埃尔哈德走过去打开了一个开关。那是雪吗？我想不是。它们在上升。是的。从风箱出来后，我们穿过另一门，进入一个摆满整齐排列的管子的房间。然后我们顺着一系列越来越窄的梯子和楼梯向上爬，越爬越高，进入更多摆着更多管子的房间。有些管子比保龄球还粗，而另一些则几乎比铅笔大不了多少。风箱里的空气变成音乐的方式是通过这些管子。每一根管子

16
00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:55.000
is tuned to a note, A, Bb, C sharp, and so on. But each pipe also has its own voice based on how it's made. It could be big and brassy, or thin and reedy. An organist decides which voices to use by opening or closing what are called stops. If the stop for the big brassy pipes is open, that's what the organ will sound like. And an organist doesn't need to choose only one stop. Multiple stops can be open all at once, so a single press of a key sends air through multiple pipes. This is where we get the phrase pulling out all the stops. I asked Klaus Ad if he could pull out all the stops for a single key.
都被调到一个音符，A、降B、升C，等等。但每根管子也根据其制造方式拥有自己的音色。它可以很大声且具有铜管乐器的音色，也可以纤细而带有芦苇般的音色。管风琴演奏者通过打开或关闭被称为“音栓”的装置来决定使用哪些音色。如果大型铜管管子对应的音栓是打开的，管风琴就会发出那种声音。而且管风琴演奏者不需要只选择一个音栓。多个音栓可以同时打开，因此只需按一下琴键，气流就会穿过多个管子。这就是我们常说的“全力以赴（pulling out all the stops，原意为拉开所有音栓）”这个短语的由来。我问克劳斯·阿德他是否可以为一个音符拉开所有音栓。

17
00:10:55.000 --> 00:11:59.000
Can you just walk me through one note as the stops open and what the sound is? Well, one stop after the other. Yeah, add each one. For instance, we're here. Or also different. And so different. And so on. And for instance I can couple. That's amazing. As long as there's an open stop, a pressed key, and a flow of air, an organ can play a note like this indefinitely.
你能带我过一遍单个音符在音栓打开时的声音变化吗？嗯，一个音栓接着一个音栓。对，每一个都加上。比如，我们在这儿。或者也可以不同。如此不同。诸如此类。比如我可以进行联动。太棒了。只要有一个打开的音栓、一个被按下的琴键以及持续的气流，管风琴就可以无限期地弹奏这样一个音符。

18
00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:34.000
So back in 1987, when Cage rewrote ASLSP specifically for the organ, his instruction to play it as slow as possible took on a whole new meaning. Cage died in 1992, but six years later, musicologists and philosophers met at a conference in Germany to determine what was the best way to play ASLSP really slowly on an organ, and how long could you possibly play it for? And they made something in Germany we called Brainstorm. Reiner Neugebauer is the chair of the board of trustees for the John Cage Organ Project.
所以在 1987 年，当凯奇专门为管风琴重写《ASLSP》时，他将其尽可能慢地演奏的指示赋予了全新的含义。凯奇于 1992 年逝世，但六年后的德国的一场会议上，音乐学家和哲学家们聚在一起，探讨在管风琴上真正缓慢演奏《ASLSP》的最佳方式是什么，以及你究竟能把它演奏多久？他们在德国创造了一个我们称之为“头脑风暴”的东西。赖纳·诺伊格鲍尔是约翰·凯奇管风琴项目理事会的主席。

19
00:12:34.000 --> 00:13:04.000
He says the conference attendees had some very different opinions, like whether the organist playing ASLSP could or should stay at the organ. And one said, oh, the organist must go to the loo or eat something. Then one religious or theologian people say, no, no, the organist must play until he die, fell dead from the seat. So it was a lifetime of the organist.
他说，与会者有非常不同的意见，比如演奏《ASLSP》的管风琴演奏者是否可以或应该一直呆在管风琴旁。其中一人说，哦，管风琴演奏者必须去上厕所或吃点东西。然后一位宗教或神学界人士说，不，不，管风琴演奏者必须一直演奏到死，从座位上倒地身亡。所以这是演奏者的一生。

20
00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:33.000
Reiner says these ideas weren't quite right, because an organist can only play a piece for so long. But the music doesn't say a person has to be the one playing it. You need not all the time an organist. You can fix the keys. In other words, you could find a way to keep the keys depressed so there's always a flow of air. No organist needed. With this approach, the only theoretical limit would be the lifespan of the organ itself.
赖纳说这些想法并不完全正确，因为演奏者只能把一首曲子演奏一段时间。但乐谱并没有说必须由一个人来演奏它。你不需要一直都有演奏者。你可以把琴键固定住。换句话说，你可以找到一种方法让琴键保持下压状态，这样就会一直有气流通过。不需要演奏者。用这种方法，唯一的理论极限就是管风琴本身的寿命。

21
00:13:33.000 --> 00:14:08.000
So when the organ breaks down, the music is over. Out of this debate came an idea to play a very long rendition of Organ 2 ASLSP on an organ made specifically for the performance. The philosophers, theologians, and musicologists also needed a place to host this indefinite concert and they landed on Halberstadt. Halberstadt was, in many ways, the perfect choice. Because it might not seem like it, but in the world of pipe organs, this rural German town of 40,000 people is kind of a big deal.
所以当管风琴损坏时，音乐就结束了。这场辩论产生了一个想法：在专门为这次演出制作的管风琴上，对《管风琴 2/ASLSP》进行一次超长时间的演绎。哲学家、神学家和音乐学家们还需要一个地方来举办这场无限期的音乐会，最终他们选中了哈尔贝施塔特。在许多方面，哈尔贝施塔特都是完美的选址。因为你可能看不出来，但在管风琴的世界里，这个拥有 4 万人口的德国乡村小镇相当有名。

22
00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:37.000
In restaurants and hotels, I kept seeing brochures that call Halberstadt, Orgelstadt, Oregon City. On the back, there was a map to eight different pipe organs around town. That's roughly one pipe organ for every 5,000 people. And the project's organizers knew about an old church in a nun's cloister called St. Bucardi that would be perfect for the performance. By 1998, the church wasn't in good shape. It had been used for just about everything but religious services since the early 19th century.
在餐馆和酒店里，我总能看到称哈尔贝施塔特为“Orgelstadt（管风琴之城）”的小册子。背面有一张标有城中八个不同管风琴的地图。这大约相当于每 5,000 人就拥有一台管风琴。项目组织者知道修女修道院里有一座名为圣布尔卡迪的古老教堂，非常适合这场演出。到 1998 年时，这座教堂的情况已经不容乐观了。自 19 世纪初以来，除了宗教仪式，它几乎被用作过各种用途。

